# Line endings: LF in the repository, LF on checkout.
#
# Without this file Git falls back to core.autocrlf, which is a per-machine
# setting -- currently true on this laptop -- so the same commit checks out as
# CRLF here and LF on the Linux CI runner. Most files are indifferent to that.
# tests/fixtures/conformance.tsv is not: it is the frozen reference verdict for
# 119 commands, replayed line by line, and it is evidence only if every reader
# sees the same bytes. Declaring the rule in the repository makes it a property
# of the project rather than a property of whoever cloned it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Git must not treat these as lines. A "line ending" inside a database file is
# a byte that happens to equal 0x0A, and rewriting it corrupts the file. None is
# tracked today; the rules are here so a future `git add -f` cannot damage one
# quietly.
*.db binary
*.sqlite binary
*.sqlite3 binary
